###Question: T – Cell Lymphomas are associated with:
###Options:
A.  a.	HTLV Type I;
B.  b.	Skin involvement;
C.  c.	Hypercalcaemia;
D.  d.	Osteolytic bone lesion;
E.  e.	Anterior mediatinal involvement.

###Answer:

outputs:
 A. a. HTLV Type I; 

###Explanation/Rationale: 
T-cell lymphomas are a group of aggressive rare lymphomas that affect T-cells. A known association exists between particular T-cell lymphomas and Human T-lymphotropic virus type I (HTLV-I). Specifically, HTLV-I is implicated in the pathogenesis of adult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma (ATLL), which is a type of T-cell lymphoma. The virus's ability to cause malignancy involves the activation of infected T-cells and subsequent clonal expansion. While skin involvement, hypercalcemia, osteolytic bone lesions, and anterior mediastinal involvement can be seen in various lymphomas, these are not specifically associated with T-cell lymphomas as universally as HTLV-I infection is with ATLL.

